ment**: MUSE (Conneau et al. 2018), VecMap (Artetxe et al. 2018) — linear mappings with 50-100 dictionary pairs. Paper claims linear projection sample efficiency is consistent with this.\n- **Representation Similarity**: CKA (Kornblith et al. 2019). Paper uses CKA for mechanistic explanation.\n- **Conformal Prediction**: Angelopoulos & Bates 2023. Paper claims complementary.\n\n## Key Claims to Evaluate for Novelty\n1. \"First systematic evaluation of cross-lingual correctness probe transfer\" — is this genuinely novel?\n2. The depth–transferability Pareto frontier concept — how novel vs. known depth-transfer tradeoffs?\n3. CKA as mechanistic explanation for WHY distributional matching fails — is this a genuine insight or post-hoc rationalization?\n4. CaCLR as a principled negative result — is it well-motivated enough to count as a contribution?\n5. Linear projection as \"constructive alternative\" — this is essentially applying VecMap/MUSE-style alignment to probe representations. How novel is this?\n6. Extending orthogonality from English to 20 languages — incremental extension or meaningful new finding?\n\n## Your Task\nWrite a detailed review covering:\n\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (novelty, positioning, clarity of contribution)\n3. **Weaknesses** (novelty concerns, missing related work, overclaiming, positioning issues)\n4. **Questions for Authors** (specific, actionable)\n5. **Minor Issues** (presentation, framing)\n6. **Overall Assessment** with score (1-6) and confidence (1-5)\n\nFocus areas for R2:\n- Is the contribution genuinely novel, or is it a systematic but incremental evaluation?\n- Is the related work coverage complete? Any important missing references?\n- Is the \"Pareto frontier\" framing novel or does prior work on depth-dependent cross-lingual transfer cover this?\n- Is CaCLR a straw man? Is MMD the right distributional matching baseline? What about other domain adaptation methods (CORAL-the-method, adversarial alignment, optimal transport)?\n- Is linear projection genuinely a contribution, or just applying known techniques (VecMap) to a new domain?\n- Does the paper overclaim (\"first systematic evaluation\") when Xiao et al. 2025 already did cross-lingual probe transfer (albeit for knowledge boundaries)?\n- Is the correctness-confidence orthogonality extension from English to 20 languages a meaningful contribution or trivial extension?\n- How does this paper position relative to LACE (Zhou et al. 2024) which already addresses multilingual calibration?\n\nBe rigorous but fair. The review should be 800-1200 words.","uuid":"8d5205fd-0323-4f0c-84ce-d50290513d72","session_id":"2e133768-be7c-4456-8949-45ac7304e840"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer R2 for a top ML venue review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 6=Strong Accept). Your focus is **novelty, positioning, and related work**.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\"Cross-Lingual Correctness Probes Reveal a Depth–Transferability Pareto Frontier: Distributional Matching Fails Where Linear Projection Succeeds\"\n\nThe paper presents the first systematic evaluation of cross-lingual correctness probe transfer across 3 architectures (Qwen3-235B-A22B MoE, Llama 4 Scout MoE, Gemma 4 31B Dense), 20+ languages, and 5 transfer methods. Key findings:\n\n1. **Pareto Frontier**: Deeper layers yield higher English AUROC (up to 0.81) but degrade in cross-lingual zero-shot transfer (median Spearman ρ = −0.52).\n2. **CKA Mechanistic Explanation**: Geometric misalignment increases with depth. CKA–transfer correlation r=0.58 (34% variance), vs MMD r=0.25 (6%).\n3. **CaCLR Negative Result**: MMD-based consistency loss is inert across 3 OOM (Cohen's d=0.08, p=0.42).\n4. **Linear Projection**: +3.5pp AUROC with ~50-100 parallel pairs, succeeds because it learns the rotation MMD cannot.\n\nAdditional findings: correctness–confidence orthogonality persists across 20 languages (cosine < 0.10), difficulty decomposition shows 65% improvement rate, cross-benchmark generalization confirmed.\n\n## Related Work as Presented in the Paper (Section 2)\n- **Probe-Based Calibration**: Burns et al. 2023, Li et al. 2024, CORAL (Miao et al. 2026), Miao & Ungar 2026 orthogonality. All English-only.\n- **Cross-Lingual Probe Transfer**: Xiao et al. 2025 (knowledge boundary probes, 8 langs — input-level answerability ≠ correctness). Civelli et al. 2024 (difficulty probes). LACE (Zhou et al. 2024) — per-language probe ensembles, needs target labels.\n- **Cross-Lingual Alignment**: MUSE (Conneau et al. 2018), VecMap (Artetxe et al. 2018) — linear mappings with 50-100 dictionary pairs. Paper claims linear projection sample efficiency is consistent with this.\n- **Representation Similarity**: CKA (Kornblith et al. 2019). Paper uses CKA for mechanistic explanation.\n- **Conformal Prediction**: Angelopoulos & Bates 2023. Paper claims complementary.\n\n## Key Claims to Evaluate for Novelty\n1. \"First systematic evaluation of cross-lingual correctness probe transfer\" — is this genuinely novel?\n2. The depth–transferability Pareto frontier concept — how novel vs. known depth-transfer tradeoffs?\n3. CKA as mechanistic explanation for WHY distributional matching fails — is this a genuine insight or post-hoc rationalization?\n4. CaCLR as a principled negative result — is it well-motivated enough to count as a contribution?\n5. Linear projection as \"constructive alternative\" — this is essentially applying VecMap/MUSE-style alignment to probe representations. How novel is this?\n6. Extending orthogonality from English to 20 languages — incremental extension or meaningful new finding?\n\n## Your Task\nWrite a detailed review covering:\n\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (novelty, positioning, clarity of contribution)\n3. **Weaknesses** (novelty concerns, missing related work, overclaiming, positioning issues)\n4. **Questions for Authors** (specific, actionable)\n5. **Minor Issues** (presentation, framing)\n6. **Overall Assessment** with score (1-6) and confidence (1-5)\n\nFocus areas for R2:\n- Is the contribution genuinely novel, or is it a systematic but incremental evaluation?\n- Is the related work coverage complete? Any important missing references?\n- Is the \"Pareto frontier\" framing novel or does prior work on depth-dependent cross-lingual transfer cover this?\n- Is CaCLR a straw man? Is MMD the right distributional matching baseline? What about other domain adaptation methods (CORAL-the-method, adversarial alignment, optimal transport)?\n- Is linear projection genuinely a contribution, or just applying known techniques (VecMap) to a new domain?\n- Does the paper overclaim (\"first systematic evaluation\") when Xiao et al. 2025 already did cross-lingual probe transfer (albeit for knowledge boundaries)?\n- Is the correctness-confidence orthogonality extension from English to 20 languages a meaningful contribution or trivial extension?\n- How does this paper position relative to LACE (Zhou et al. 2024) which already addresses multilingual calibration?\n\nBe rigorous but fair. The review should be 800-1200 words."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01UC21HHPGCD6Y8MsSPEacMe","session_id":"2e133768-be7c-4456-8949-45ac7304e840","uuid":"cb62acce-c560-4150-9c09-e550b12553e1","timestamp":"2026-05-20T21:06:39.825Z"}
